 which was about five  blocks away from spanish harlem but it's  a world apart  so  around that time as i um i discovered  comic books now as i mentioned  uh earlier  i i did start speaking english at a late  age so  as a result my reading levels were  always low  so  as i continue to read comic books  mom  like one summer in particular  my reading levels jumped two grades  so when i took the  reading comprehension test in school  the teachers thought that i might have  been cheating so they gave me the test a  second time  and of course i scored higher the second  time around  as a res uh as a result comic books  became a big part of my life i began  reading them a lot and i started to draw  my own based on drawing draw my own  stories based on my friends and  movies that my father would take me to  i just was you know having a ball at  that point  up until the time i was a senior in high  school i wanted to be a comic book  artist  but  when i was a senior that's when i was  introduced to oil painting at the high  school of art design by two wonderful  teachers  max ginsberg and erwin irwin greenberg  i then attended college at the  school of visual arts where i continued  to develop my painting technique  i began my career as an illustrator  right after receiving my bfa from the  school of visual arts  and for the first 12 years of my career  i concentrated mostly on doing book  covers and chapter books  then i got a chance to illustrate my  first picture book the piano man  for walker and company  the experience of creating the images  for the piano man reminded me  of my childhood when i would create my  own comic books  i loved it  so around after after completing my  fourth picture book of grandma's records  which i wrote and is based on my summers  with my grandmother in spanish harlem  walker and company asked me if i would  be interested in illustrating champion  the story of muhammad ali  i immediately became excited at the idea  of illustrating the life 